  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAs we go into next week our attention turns to the west and \nsouthwest. Large trough takes shape across the western CONUS Monday. \nMeanwhile, Hurricane Polo off the west coast of Mexico lifts north \nearly in the week. Its remnant moisture looks to phase with the \ntrough as we into Tuesday. Moisture plume ahead of these features \nwill bring increasing rain chances to the area by Tuesday as well. \n\nUncertainty increases beyond this point as models diverge with \nregards to timing. Although both the GFS and the Euro show a closed \nlow, the Euro is farther south and becomes a bit more cut off from \nthe main flow and is therefore slower with its eastward progress \nthrough the week. The Euro's trough and the remnants of Polo also \nrotate around each other over Arizona and New Mexico before ejecting \nout by Friday. Meanwhile, the GFS is more progressive and has the \nsystem coming through late Wednesday and Wednesday night with a more \nspring-time severe weather look. Either way looks to bring the best \nchance of widespread beneficial rainfall that we have seen in quite \nsome time.",
